    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#david"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david.jpg" alt="David" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David,_the_King_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerard van Honthorst, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> grew out of the Christian principalities of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> that re-emerged as Arab power receded in the ninth and tenth centuries. In the south-west the Bagratid princes of Tao-Klarjeti revived the title of king of the Iberians in 888, while in the west the Kingdom of Abkhazia, ruled from Kutaisi, had become a Georgian-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> realm. The decisive figure in their union was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a> III Kuropalates of Tao, the most powerful Bagratid prince of his day, who adopted his young kinsman Bagrat and secured him a throne in Kartli in the 970s. Bagrat inherited the Kingdom of Abkhazia through his mother in 978, and when his father Gurgen died in 1008 he united western and eastern Georgia as Bagrat III. He also brought Kakheti and Hereti under his rule around 1010, though they recovered their independence after his death in 1014, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emirate">emirate</a> of Tbilisi remained in Muslim hands. The young kingdom was squeezed between two powers: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, which annexed Tao after David III died in 1000 and defeated George I in 1021–1022, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, whose invasions from the 1060s and the so-called Great Turkish Troubles of the 1080s reduced George II to paying tribute.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerusalem.jpg" alt="Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Mount_(Aerial_view,_2007)_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Georgian society was organised around landholding and service. At the top stood the great nobles, the <em>didebulni</em> and the <em>eristavi</em> who governed provinces, below them the lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gentry">gentry</a> or <em>aznauri</em>, and at the base a peasantry that was becoming increasingly dependent on lords and monasteries. Agriculture rested on grain in the lowlands of Kartli and Kakheti, on vineyards, which were central to Georgian life and liturgy, and on stock-raising in the mountains. Trade grew as the kingdom expanded, because Tbilisi, taken in 1122 and made the capital, lay on routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a>, the Caspian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a>; tolls and the tribute of Muslim neighbours such as the Shirvanshahs enriched the crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a> IV and his successors struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> coins bearing Arabic as well as Georgian legends, a sign of a mixed urban world. Tbilisi had large Muslim, Armenian and Jewish communities, and Muslim writers reported that David IV protected the Muslims of the city and treated their scholars with respect. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a> was the largest landowner after the crown, and Georgian monasteries flourished abroad, notably the Iviron on Mount Athos, the Monastery of the Cross in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and Petritsoni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, linking the kingdom to the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#alp-arslan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alp-arslan.jpg" alt="Alp Arslan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alp_Arslan_on_throne_Majma_al-Tawarikh_by_Hafiz_Abru_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hafiz-i Abru, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early kingdom fought defensively, losing border lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and suffering repeated Seljuk invasions after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>’s campaign of 1068. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a> IV the Builder (r. 1089–1125) transformed its military position. He stopped paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in the 1090s, annexed Kakheti and Hereti in 1104, and built a standing force loyal to the crown, including a royal guard known as the <em>monaspa</em>. Around 1118 he invited Kipchak nomads from the steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a>, traditionally 40,000 families, settling them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> as a professional mounted army; David had married a Kipchak khan’s daughter. On 12 August 1121, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-didgori">Battle of Didgori</a>, his army routed a large Muslim coalition led by the Artuqid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilghazi">Ilghazi</a>, and in 1122 he captured Tbilisi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-iii">George III</a> took Ani in 1161, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a> the generals Zakare and Ivane Mkhargrdzeli won at Shamkor in 1195 and at Basiani around 1202, where they defeated the army of the Seljuk sultan of Rum, Rukn al-Din Süleymanshah. Georgian forces then raided deep into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>. This strength proved brittle: the Mongol vanguard of Jebe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#subutai">Subutai</a> defeated George IV at Khunan in 1221, and the Khwarazmian ruler Jalal al-Din sacked Tbilisi in 1226.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#george-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-iii.jpg" alt="George III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#george-iii">George III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allan_Ramsay_-_King_George_III_in_coronation_robes_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allan Ramsay, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> was a monarchy whose kings styled themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#negus-nagast">king of kings</a> and ruled through a royal council, the <em>darbazi</em>, and a small group of high officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a> IV strengthened the centre by creating the office of <em>mtsignobartukhutsesi-chqondideli</em>, which combined the posts of chancellor and archbishop of Chqondidi and placed a royal appointee at the head of both administration and church affairs. The Council of Ruisi-Urbnisi in 1103 reformed the church and removed hostile prelates. Other great offices included the commander-in-chief, or <em>amirspasalari</em>, the majordomo and the treasurer. Succession passed within the Bagratid dynasty, which claimed descent from the biblical King David. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-iii">George III</a> made his daughter Tamar co-ruler in 1178, and on his death in 1184 she reigned in her own right with the title of king. The great nobles tried to limit her power, and early in her reign she had to concede them greater influence in government, while the Armenian-born Zakarid brothers rose to command the army and govern the conquered Armenian lands around Ani. Provincial power rested with hereditary <em>eristavi</em>, whose ambitions repeatedly threatened unity. In the fourteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-v">George V</a> the Brilliant issued a law code for the mountain districts, regulating feuds and compensation, as part of his restoration of royal authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#david-iv-of-georgia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia.jpg" alt="David IV of Georgia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#david-iv-of-georgia">David IV of Georgia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_IV,_detail_from_icon,_St._Catherine_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Certain types of Egyptian works (official documents, stamps, works of national folklore) should use specific license ..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s age of greatness, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a> IV to Tamar, left a deep mark on Georgian identity. It was the period of Shota Rustaveli’s epic <em>The Knight in the Panther’s Skin</em>, written around 1200 and dedicated to Tamar, which became the national poem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a>. David IV founded the Gelati monastery near Kutaisi in 1106 with an academy where scholars such as Ioane Petritsi and Arsen Iqaltoeli translated and commented on Greek theology and philosophy. The rock-cut monastery of Vardzia, consecrated in the 1180s, the Bagrati Cathedral at Kutaisi and the rebuilt Svetitskhoveli Cathedral at Mtskheta, finished around 1029, show the ambition of Georgian church architecture, while goldsmiths and fresco painters produced works of high quality. Historians compiled the chronicles later gathered as <em>Kartlis Tskhovreba</em>, the Life of Georgia. Beyond its borders the kingdom dominated Shirvan and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>, and Tamar helped her kinsmen Alexios and David Komnenos seize Trebizond in 1204, founding the Empire of Trebizond. It sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> institutions from Athos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Later Georgians looked back to the united kingdom as the model of statehood; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david-iv-of-georgia">David the Builder</a> and Tamar were canonised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a> and remain central figures of national memory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#david-vi-narin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-vi-narin.jpg" alt="David VI Narin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#david-vi-narin">David VI Narin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Narin,_Gelati_Monastery,_south-east_chapel,_west_wall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s decline began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a>. Between 1236 and 1243 Mongol armies overran the country, and Queen Rusudan accepted Mongol overlordship. The Mongols divided authority between two co-kings named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david">David</a>, and in 1259–1260 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david-vi-narin">David VI Narin</a> rebelled and withdrew to Kutaisi, creating the separate Kingdom of Imereti in the west. Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> remained under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, which executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II</a> in 1289. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-v">George V</a> the Brilliant restored the kingdom’s fortunes: he took advantage of turmoil in the Ilkhanate from the late 1320s to end Mongol rule, reunited Imereti in 1330 and brought the princes of Samtskhe back under the crown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-death">Black Death</a> in the 1340s and then the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>, who took Tbilisi in 1386 and invaded repeatedly until 1403, devastated the population, churches and towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> the Great (r. 1412–1442) rebuilt churches and fortifications, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a> ruler Jahan Shah sacked Tbilisi in 1440, and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453 cut Georgia off from Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Regional lords pressed for independence: Bagrat of Imereti defeated George VIII at Chikhori in 1463, and the atabeg Qvarqvare II of Samtskhe captured him in 1465. In 1490 a royal council under Constantine II accepted the division into the kingdoms of Kartli, Kakheti and Imereti and the Principality of Samtskhe.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a> united the Christian lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> in 1008 and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david-iv-of-georgia">David the Builder</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a>, became the region’s dominant power and the setting of a cultural golden age, before Mongol conquest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>’s invasions and noble separatism broke it into rival kingdoms by 1490.</em></p>

